- op: discuss  # 4997
  at: '2026-09-11T10:18:36Z'  # 4997
  by: agent  # 4997
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 4997
  clock: 3  # 4997
  nonce: '4997'  # 4997
  message: "THE FINDING-WEIGHTED NUMBER THIS ITEM ASKED FOR, FIRST DATA POINT (arc T8).\nRecord: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/jivih_t8_09112026/ (`size_findings.py`,\n`RESULT.md`). Computed from artifacts produced for T0c and T7; no new analysis\nrun was needed.\n\nThe item says: \"SIZED FINDING-WEIGHTED FIRST: 24 of 324 is a share of NAMES;\nthe share of FINDINGS is unmeasured and decides whether this earns a field or a\ndated declared blindness.\" The 2026-09-06 concept audit had already recorded\n\"Harm, honestly. Unmeasured.\"\n\n    repo                        findings  choice-shaped    share\n    pretix (python)                  125              0     0.0%\n    workadventure (ts/js)             18              0     0.0%\n    modsecurity (cpp/c)                0              0      n/a\n    apollo-server (ts)                 2              0     0.0%\n    TOTAL                            145              0     0.0%\n\n**0 of 145 findings carry a choice-shaped source.**\n\nAND THE ZERO IS CHECKED AGAINST A MISSING POPULATION, which is the whole risk\nhere. A zero has two causes licensing opposite conclusions: the rows exist and\nnever produce findings (the property is harmless), or the rows are never called\nin this corpus (the corpus cannot see the question). So io-boundary CHAINS were\ncounted too, independently of findings: workadventure 1585 chains / 0\nchoice-shaped, modsecurity 61 / 1, apollo-server 61 / 0. The single hit is\n`sys/wait.wait` on modsecurity, and it sits on the **`subprocess`** boundary --\na SINK -- not on one of the three source boundaries that mint\n`untrusted_input`, so it is not even an instance of the property.\n\n**THIS CORPUS CANNOT ANSWER THE QUESTION AND THE 0.0% MUST NOT BE READ AS \"THE\nPROPERTY IS HARMLESS\".** 17 of the 24 choice-shaped names live in go, java,\nerlang and rust -- `accept` in three of them, the whole erlang `inet` DNS\nfamily in the fourth -- and this corpus contains no repository in any of those\nlanguages. It was selected for a different measurement.\n\nWHAT A REAL SIZING NEEDS: a cohort with at least one SERVER in go, java, erlang\nand rust, run through `verify-claims` with the generic claims, counting findings\nwhose `source_primitives` last segment is in the choice set. The instrument is\nwritten, takes any set of `vc.json` artifacts, and runs in under a second; only\nthe cohort is missing.\n\nPREDICTION RECORDED BEFORE THAT COHORT RUNS, so it cannot be adjusted\nafterwards: if a corpus that CAN see the population also returns ~0 findings,\nthen a schema field on 324 catalogue rows is paid for by nothing and the dated\ndeclared blindness is the honest answer. This data point narrows the decision;\nit does not make it."  # 4997
- op: discuss  # 9b5d
  at: '2026-09-11T11:10:31Z'  # 9b5d
  by: agent  # 9b5d
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 9b5d
  clock: 4  # 9b5d
  nonce: 9b5d  # 9b5d
  message: "THE COHORT THE LAST ENTRY ASKED FOR HAS RUN, AND ITS PRE-REGISTERED FALSIFIER FIRED. Record: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/jivih_t8_09112026/ (PLAN-COHORT.md written before the run, RESULT-COHORT.md, size_cohort.py, cohort/).\n\nThe previous entry said a real sizing needs 'a cohort with at least one SERVER in go, java, erlang and rust'. That cohort is coredns (go, a DNS server), cowboy (erlang, an HTTP server), robyn (rust, a web server) and killbill (java, a server-side platform).\n\n    POPULATION -- chains naming a choice-shaped primitive\n      coredns  145 chains -> 0        cowboy   74 chains -> 0\n      robyn    141 chains -> 2        killbill 202 chains -> 0\n      TOTAL    562 chains -> 2        (robyn: socket.gethostname, socket.gethostbyname)\n    FINDINGS   21 total -> 0 choice-shaped (0.0%), reported VOID per the pre-registration\n\nTHE ANALYZERS WERE NOT IDLE: coredns reached 143 go chains across 10 boundary kinds, cowboy 74 erlang chains across 10, killbill 40 java. These are working surveys, not empty runs.\n\n**AND THE OBVIOUS EXPLANATION WAS WRONG, WHICH IS THE FINDING.** The striking line looked like 'an Erlang HTTP server whose whole job is gen_tcp:accept reached zero', and that would have been a recall defect worth filing. Checked in the source BEFORE publishing: cowboy has ZERO gen_tcp:accept call sites (socket handling is delegated to RANCH), coredns has ZERO .Accept()/.ReadFrom( (it uses net.Listen and hands off to the dns.Server library), killbill has ZERO .accept() (a servlet application; the CONTAINER accepts). The erlang catalogue does carry the rows, so it is not a catalogue gap either. **All four zeros are CORRECT REFUSALS.**\n\nWHAT THE COHORT ESTABLISHED IS NOT WHAT IT SET OUT TO. It was chosen by PROTOCOL ROLE, and that is the wrong selector: a modern network server almost never owns its socket accept loop -- a library or a container does. So **the accept family (10 of the 24 names) is concentrated in code that first-party analysis structurally does not read.** The other two thirds behave differently and lumping them is what made both attempts look alike: the DNS family (7) and exit status (7) ARE called by applications, and robyn's two hits are exactly that.\n\nBEARING ON THIS ITEM'S DECISION: it STRENGTHENS the dated-declared-blindness option for the accept third, for a reason nobody had stated -- a per-row value_shape field on ten rows first-party analysis essentially never reaches is paid for by nothing however the rows are labelled. It says much LESS about the DNS and exit-status thirds, which this cohort barely sampled (2 chains).\n\nNOT ESTABLISHED: four repositories, and the accept claim rests on three grep-level checks rather than a survey of how server code is written. **The right next cohort is selected by 'does this repo own its socket loop' -- a load balancer, a protocol implementation, an embedded server -- not by 'is this a server'.**"  # 9b5d
- op: discuss  # 79f3
  at: '2026-09-11T11:15:19Z'  # 79f3
  by: agent  # 79f3
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 79f3
  clock: 5  # 79f3
  nonce: 79f3  # 79f3
  message: "CORRECTION TO MY PREVIOUS ENTRY, from a second cohort run to attack my own explanation.\n\nThe previous entry explained the null result by saying the accept family is 'concentrated in code that first-party analysis structurally does not read' and 'essentially never a first-party call site in an application repository'. **THAT WORDING IS TOO STRONG AND THE TEST I RAN TO CHECK IT SAYS SO.**\n\nSecond cohort, chosen by WHAT THE CODE OWNS rather than by protocol role -- repositories with direct socket-call sites in their own source (passt, a user-mode TCP/IP stack, 11 sites; torsocks, 10) against 0 in coredns and gin. Prediction recorded before the run: if my explanation is right, these reach choice-shaped primitives where the four servers did not; if they also return ~0, my explanation is wrong and must be replaced.\n\n    repo       chains  choice-shaped   net_* rows reached\n    passt         196        3         recvfrom, getaddrinfo, ACCEPT, recv,\n                                       recvmsg, connect, send, sendmsg\n    torsocks       18        0         (none -- an LD_PRELOAD interposer whose\n                                       source wraps symbols rather than calling\n                                       them; neither confirmation nor counterexample)\n\n**sys/socket.accept IS REACHED on passt (2 chains), plus unistd.gethostname.** So the four servers' zeros were correct refusals BECAUSE THOSE REPOSITORIES DELEGATE, and the explanation survived a test that could have killed it.\n\nTHE ACCURATE STATEMENT, replacing the one above: accept IS reachable and IS reached, in the MINORITY of repositories that own their socket loop; it is absent from the majority that delegate to a library, framework or container. The population is not unreachable -- it is CONCENTRATED in a structurally identifiable subset, and a corpus lacking that subset measures zero and means nothing by it.\n\nWHAT THAT DOES TO THIS ITEM'S DECISION: a value_shape field on the accept rows is NOT paid for by nothing. It is paid for by whatever share of a real corpus owns its sockets -- a number this session has not measured, and the one the decision actually turns on. My earlier prediction (that a corpus which can see the population would also return ~0) is NOT yet confirmed and should not be treated as if it were: passt is a corpus that can see it, and it returned 3."  # 79f3
- op: discuss  # f0aa
  at: '2026-09-11T11:25:05Z'  # f0aa
  by: agent  # f0aa
  actor: jgstern_agent  # f0aa
  clock: 6  # f0aa
  nonce: f0aa  # f0aa
  message: "THE SHARE I SAID NOBODY HAD RUN, RUN. My previous entry ended 'what is unmeasured is what share of a real corpus owns its sockets, and that is the number this decision actually turns on'. Bound on it, from a source-level census over ~/whole_bunch_of_repos (one bounded grep per repo, accept family across c/cpp/go/java/python/rust/erlang, excluding tests/vendor/third_party):\n\n    repositories scanned                                        270\n    with a DIRECT accept-family call site in first-party source 104\n    SHARE                                                      38.5%\n\nSPOT-CHECKED 5 of the 104, because a loose pattern returning a plausible number is the exact failure this project keeps recording: containerd l.Accept(), docker-cli pl.accept(), spark listen_sock.accept() in pyspark/daemon.py, slirp4netns accept(listenfd,...) in C, okhttp (a samples/ file). **5 of 5 genuine.** The pattern is not mostly noise.\n\nIT IS AN UPPER BOUND AND IT OVERSTATES: only 5 of 104 read back; the pattern matches a METHOD DEFINITION as well as a call, and counts a samples/ hit; and it says nothing about whether hypergumbo REACHES the row at that site (the R2 module-context route can still refuse it).\n\nWHAT IT DOES TO THE STORY, and it is a further correction to me: my first explanation ('libraries own it, applications do not') is directionally right for the four servers measured and WRONG as a general claim. The four-server cohort was not merely mis-selected -- it was UNREPRESENTATIVE, since coredns, cowboy, killbill and robyn all happen to sit in the 61.5%. **The accept rows are reachable in a LARGE MINORITY of real repositories**, which is a materially different input to this decision than either 'essentially never fires' or 'unreachable'.\n\nHONEST SUMMARY OF THREE COHORTS AND A CENSUS: the property's reachability is a PER-REPOSITORY fact with wide variance, and every number this session produced for it is a floor or a bound on a corpus not chosen for it. The decision still needs a finding-weighted number on a corpus sampled deliberately for socket ownership -- and what has changed is that such a corpus is now known to be easy to build, with 104 candidates named."  # f0aa
